                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1233,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1016,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Takes a run of consecutive positions along one dimension: start here, take this many.\n\nt.narrow with dim 0, start 2, length 3 gives rows 2, 3 and 4 - the same as t[] 2:5 - but the three numbers sit in boxes you can drag or drive from the patch, which a typed slice cannot. Use it for a moving window over frames, a band of rows, a block of channels.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.narrow <dim: int> <start: int> <length: int>\nDefaults: dim 0, start 0, length 1. t.narrow 0 2 3 takes rows 2, 3 and\n4; t.narrow 1 0 4 the first four columns.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.narrow 0 2 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the run on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dimension to cut along: 0 rows, 1 columns. Negative counts from the end.\n\nstart (drag int, also an inlet):\nThe first position kept. Negative counts from the end (-3 is three from the end). Default 0, or the second argument.\n\nlength (drag int, also an inlet):\nHow many positions to keep. Default 1, or the third argument.\n\nChanging dim, start or length does NOT send by itself - the next tensor uses them; in the demo a metro inside make_numbered re-sends the source ten times a second. The run must fit: a start past the end, a length of 0 or less, or a run that would go past the end sends nothing and prints an error in the console - once per tensor, so with a streaming source the console fills until you bring the numbers back in range.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe run. The dimension is KEPT (length 1 gives one row, still a matrix). It is a view of the input, not a copy.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe left heat_map is the numbered source - tens digit the row, units digit the column. t.narrow 0 2 3 starts on rows 2, 3 and 4 (20 to 49). Drag start and the band of rows slides; drag length and it widens. Set dim to 1 and it cuts columns instead.\n\nRELATED:\nt[] does the same with a typed slice (2:5), and more. t.select takes one position and removes the dimension. t.chunk cuts a tensor into equal pieces. np.crop cuts a rectangle in numpy.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1000,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Takes a run of consecutive positions along one dimension: start here, take this many.\n\nt.narrow with dim 0, start 2, length 3 gives rows 2, 3 and 4 - the same as t[] 2:5 - but the three numbers sit in boxes you can drag or drive from the patch, which a typed slice cannot. Use it for a moving window over frames, a band of rows, a block of channels.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.narrow <dim: int> <start: int> <length: int>\nDefaults: dim 0, start 0, length 1. t.narrow 0 2 3 takes rows 2, 3 and\n4; t.narrow 1 0 4 the first four columns.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.narrow 0 2 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the run on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dimension to cut along: 0 rows, 1 columns. Negative counts from the end.\n\nstart (drag int, also an inlet):\nThe first position kept. Negative counts from the end (-3 is three from the end). Default 0, or the second argument.\n\nlength (drag int, also an inlet):\nHow many positions to keep. Default 1, or the third argument.\n\nChanging dim, start or length does NOT send by itself - the next tensor uses them; in the demo a metro inside make_numbered re-sends the source ten times a second. The run must fit: a start past the end, a length of 0 or less, or a run that would go past the end sends nothing and prints an error in the console - once per tensor, so with a streaming source the console fills until you bring the numbers back in range.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe run. The dimension is KEPT (length 1 gives one row, still a matrix). It is a view of the input, not a copy.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe left heat_map is the numbered source - tens digit the row, units digit the column. t.narrow 0 2 3 starts on rows 2, 3 and 4 (20 to 49). Drag start and the band of rows slides; drag length and it widens. Set dim to 1 and it cuts columns instead.\n\nRELATED:\nt[] does the same with a typed slice (2:5), and more. t.select takes one position and removes the dimension. t.chunk cuts a tensor into equal pieces. np.crop cuts a rectangle in numpy."
                },
